# Local environment files.
# The project-generated .env holds only PUBLIC Lovable Cloud config
# (URL, publishable key, project id, release-control flags) and is
# tracked in Git so fresh runtimes boot without manual restoration.
# Local override variants remain ignored.
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Supabase CLI local-stack artefacts
supabase/.branches/
supabase/.temp/

# Regulatory source monitor output. The REPORT is a per-run artefact and is
# regenerated on every check; the BASELINE next to it is tracked deliberately,
# because "what we last accepted as the state of the law" is exactly the kind
# of thing that must be reviewable in a diff.
/regulatory-source-report.md

# Local-only Playwright override (browser path); never committed.
pw.local.config.ts
pw.shots.mjs

# `bun run cv-export:proof` writes real PDFs here so a human can look at the
# thing a candidate would send. Regenerated on demand from the fixtures in the
# script, so the files themselves are evidence rather than source.
# Written as `/artifacts/*` rather than `/artifacts/`: git does not descend
# into an excluded DIRECTORY, so a negation for something inside one never
# matches. Excluding the contents instead leaves the directory itself
# traversable, which is what makes the exception below possible at all.
/artifacts/*

# ONE EXCEPTION, on review's explicit request.
#
# The E1 continuity captures are committed so the browser evidence is readable
# in the pull request itself rather than only on the machine that produced it:
# several of the states they show -- report material beside a finalised report,
# a failed read that does not become a zero, a standalone process -- are the
# whole point of the change and cannot be read off the diff.
#
# They are regenerated, not hand-made: INDEX.md carries the exact HEAD, the
# fixture commands and the Playwright command, and re-running it against the
# same tree reproduces them. A directory is re-included before its contents,
# because git cannot un-ignore a file inside an excluded directory.
!/artifacts/employer-continuity-e1/

# The E4 final-report captures, for the same reason and under the same rules:
# artifacts/employer-final-report-e4/INDEX.md carries the HEAD, the fixture
# commands and the Playwright command that regenerate them.
!/artifacts/employer-final-report-e4/
# ...except the manifest, which every evidence RUN produces. It is a record of
# one workflow run -- its HEAD, its toolchain, its file digests -- and belongs
# in that run's artifact, not in the repository, where a stale copy would
# describe a commit nobody is looking at.
/artifacts/employer-final-report-e4/manifest.json

# The BESKT PR 3 candidate-preparation captures, under the same rules as E1
# and E4 above: committed so a reviewer can READ the Swedish and English
# screens at both widths in the pull request itself. Several of the states
# they show -- the nine notices, a skipped question presented with no warning
# styling, the read-only confirmation that offers no correction control -- are
# the point of the change and cannot be read off the diff.
#
# Regenerated, never hand-made. INDEX.md carries the exact HEAD and the three
# commands that reproduce them, and manifest.json carries the SHA-256 of every
# file plus the per-phase durations.
!/artifacts/beskt-candidate-preparation/

# The captures themselves, the manifest and the index -- not the inputs they
# were rendered from. The HTML fragments and the copied production stylesheet
# are regenerated by `bun run beskt-candidate-preparation-render:check` and
# would only be a stale second copy of the build in the diff.
/artifacts/beskt-candidate-preparation/*.html
/artifacts/beskt-candidate-preparation/styles.css
/artifacts/beskt-candidate-preparation/live/
